WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
How do you listen to hundreds of neurons firing at once?

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.780
One electrode in one cell catches only a single voice.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:14.280
Yet waves, oscillations, and odour patterns belong to whole populations acting

4
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:15.280
together.

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:22.760
Instead of one electrode in one cell, tissue rests on an array or holds a probe.

6
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:30.040
The original Neuropixels probe from 2017 carries 960 recording sites.

7
00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:34.040
All sit along one shank about 10 mm long.

8
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.800
Push it into a mouse brain, and you hear spikes from hundreds of neurons at once.

9
00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:45.893
Hearing many neurons at once reveals a population pattern, a list of each cell's

10
00:00:45.893 --> 00:00:52.373
response. When a mouse smells odour A, one neuron fires, one stays quiet, and

11
00:00:52.373 --> 00:00:53.493
another fires a little.

12
00:00:54.813 --> 00:00:57.733
Do the same for odour B, and compare both lists.

13
00:00:58.873 --> 00:01:03.853
Two odours are represented similarly when many of the same neurons respond to both.

14
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:08.587
To build that list, sites feed channels, then sorted units.

15
00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:13.667
Spike sorting groups spikes by shape, each unit meant to be one neuron.

16
00:01:14.987 --> 00:01:19.207
Yet 960 sites do not equal 960 neurons.

17
00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:24.147
One neuron can reach several sites; one site can hear several.

18
00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:30.267
Quality scores catch only some errors; a unit can still merge or split neurons.

19
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:34.993
Now check the next link in the chain, from units to animals.

20
00:01:36.613 --> 00:01:40.133
Suppose a study records 300 sorted units from three mice.

21
00:01:41.473 --> 00:01:43.453
How many samples does it actually have?

22
00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:55.637
Three, not 300. More units sharpen each animal's estimate, but they all still come

23
00:01:55.637 --> 00:01:56.637
from the same mouse.

24
00:01:57.857 --> 00:02:02.877
Only more animals add samples, so trustworthy analysis works at the animal level.

25
00:02:04.277 --> 00:02:09.417
Arrays hear populations together, but sites are not neurons, and sorted units are

26
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:10.417
not animals.

27
00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:17.327
In 1991, Meister, Wong, Baylor, and Shatz laid isolated retinas on an array.

28
00:02:18.527 --> 00:02:24.227
They recorded up to 100 ganglion cells at once from newborn ferrets and fetal cats.

29
00:02:25.687 --> 00:02:30.687
Nearby cells fired in nearly synchronous bursts, waves several hundred micrometres

30
00:02:30.687 --> 00:02:38.407
wide. In 2012, Ackman, Burbridge, and Crair imaged live neonatal mice, showing waves

31
00:02:38.407 --> 00:02:39.507
are no dish artefact.

32
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.440
Arrays also track cortical organoids, tissue grown in a dish, for months.

33
00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:52.120
In 2019, Trujillo, Muotri, and colleagues recorded these organoids on electrode

34
00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:58.560
arrays. They reported network oscillations growing more complex, with some features

35
00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:03.120
resembling brain-wave recordings, EEG, from preterm infants.

36
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.940
Yet coordinated firing in tissue with no inputs is a long way from a working brain.

37
00:03:11.083 --> 00:03:16.483
From retinal waves to organoid rhythms, arrays and probes reveal how whole

38
00:03:16.483 --> 00:03:17.883
populations act together.

39
00:03:19.223 --> 00:03:22.543
Still, big recording numbers demand care at every step.

40
00:03:23.863 --> 00:03:28.983
Count real neurons, analyse at the animal level, and match every claim to what was

41
00:03:28.983 --> 00:03:29.363
measured.
